Copyleft components run as separate processes or containers, with ONE exception
that a redistributor has to know about:

  * SimQN (GPL-3.0) is imported into the bb84-kme service's own Python process.
      - services/bb84-kme/Dockerfile runs
        `pip install -c constraints.txt -e /opt/SimQN`, so it is baked into the
        image;
      - services/bb84-kme/app/backends/simqn_backend.py imports `qns` and
        `qns.entity.QNode` and runs them in-process, not over a socket;
      - config/qkd_params.yaml sets `backend: "simqn"` as the DEFAULT, so the
        default build exercises it.
    Treat services/bb84-kme as a GPL-3.0 sub-component and be prepared to make
    the corresponding source offer if you hand that image to a third party.
    Selecting SIMULATOR_BACKEND=cvqkd or qutip stops SimQN being INVOKED; it
    does not remove it from an image already built.

Until 2026-08-27 this paragraph read "None of the copyleft components is linked
into this repository's own binaries; they run as separate processes or
containers." That was false for SimQN in the default configuration, and
docs/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md already said the opposite ("used as a
Python-importable library inside services/bb84-kme"). The two licence documents
disagreed and this was the wrong one; a downstream redistributor relying on the
mere-aggregation argument would have shipped the default image carrying an
unrecognised source-offer obligation.

Everything else -- qkdnetsim (GPL-2.0) and the strongSwan lane -- does run out
of process. That separation is what keeps the rest of the combination
distributable, so it is a constraint on how this project may be integrated
rather than a footnote. See docs/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md for per-dependency
detail, including the npm packages.

